    "question": {
      ":question/id": "111755c5-cf23-571f-80b3-7d2188af00e9",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider a circle. Let $A$ denote the area and let $d$ denote the diameter. The diameter can vary. What is the best interpretation of ${A}^{\u2032}(d)$?",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The function $A(d)$ gives the area of the circle as a function of the diameter. So,\n\n$$\n{A}^{\u2032}(d)=\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}d}=\\frac{\\text{Instantaneous change in }A}{\\text{Change in }d}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${A}^{\u2032}(d)$ is the rate at which the area changes as the diameter changes.",
